    "We want a prediction at every time step, so the output sequence is as long as the input.": "clips/3643c1e021f9d00e.mp3",
    "There is one rule. The prediction at time t may only use the inputs up to time t. No peeking into the future.": "clips/7a8c5e6003b49cbf.mp3",
    "This property is called causality. As t moves forward, the set of allowed inputs grows, but it never reaches past t.": "clips/bfda9323d8fa42d7.mp3",
    "An RNN is causal by construction. A convolutional network gets there with two ideas, and becomes deep with a third.": "clips/946b93ec73e6ec3f.mp3",
    "Start with an ordinary one-dimensional convolution: a kernel of three weights slides along the sequence.": "clips/27782eeef90b3102.mp3",
    "Each output is a weighted sum of neighbouring inputs. With a centred kernel, that's this formula.": "clips/cc69f50bc3888128.mp3",
    "But look at the last term. x at t plus one lies in the future: a centred kernel leaks information backwards in time.": "clips/c4da77b3770b2af6.mp3",
    "The fix: shift the kernel so it only looks backwards. Now y at time t uses x at t minus two, t minus one, and t.": "clips/5e7856305cf144e5.mp3",
    "At the very start there's nothing to look back at, so we pad the left end with k minus one zeros: two of them. Now the output has exactly the same length as the input.": "clips/3ffac45ac8a0b8c6.mp3",
    "That's a causal convolution: every output depends only on the present and the past.": "clips/da09e921c6948387.mp3",
    "Stack several causal layers and each output can see further back. How far back it sees is called its receptive field.": "clips/60616de8e3a0e830.mp3",
